r have a weaker hand than yours.\n\nBut could they have a flush?\n\nYes — any **club** gives them a flush. Since there are four clubs, even a low club like 7♣, 6♣, etc., would give them a flush.\n\nBut how likely is it they have a club?\n\n- Pre-flop check: unlikely they have premium hand like AK suited.\n- They didn’t bet or raise on flop or turn — despite multiple clubs.\n\nOn flop: Q♣, 8♣, 10♦ → two clubs — if they had a flush draw (e.g., A♣X), they might bet or raise for initiative, but they checked.\n\nThen on turn: 9♣ comes — third club appears (now three clubs: Q, 8, 9, plus turn adds another club? Wait — turn card was 9♣, so after turn: Q♣, 8♣, 10♦, 9♣ → three clubs.\n\nThen river: K♣ → fourth club.\n\nWait — total clubs on board: Q♣, 8♣, 9♣, K♣ → **four clubs**.\n\nSo flush is possible.\n\nBut opponent never bet — always checked or called.\n\nThey called your **100 raise on the turn** — when the board had three clubs and a possible straight draw.\n\nThat suggests they may have:\n- A made hand like two pair, set, or straight themselves.\n- Or a flush draw they’re calling with.\n\nBut on the river, they **checked again** — if they made the flush, why not bet? Many players will bet or raise for value.\n\nTheir river check suggests:\n- They may not have hit the flush.\n- Or they are slow-playing — but unlikely given earlier passivity.\n\nMore likely: they have a **weaker hand** than a flush — perhaps two pair (like Q-8), or a lower straight? But lower straight isn't possible — the only straight is 9-T-J-Q-K.\n\nWait — could someone else have the same straight?\n\nYes — anyone with a Jack has the same straight. There are four Jacks — you have one — so three left.\n\nBut also, **if they have J♣**, they have the **same straight AND a flush** — which would beat you.\n\nBut again, they’ve been passive.\n\nGiven the action, it’s unlikely they have the nuts (flush or straight-flush). More likely they have:\n- Two pair (e.g., Q-8, K-8)\n- A weaker one pair\n- Or a busted draw.\n\nTherefore, your **straight is likely the best hand**.\n\nNow, should you **check back (0)** or **bet for value (e.g., 100 or more)**?\n\nIf you check, you risk losing nothing but may win only a small pot.\n\nIf you bet, you can **extract value** from worse hands that might call — like:\n- Two pair\n- Top pair\n- A missed flush draw that calls out of curiosity\n\nBut if they have a flush, they might raise — and you’d have to fold.\n\nBut again, likelihood of flush is moderate, and their passivity suggests they don’t.\n\nBest strategy: **Bet for value** — but not too big.\n\nYou already raised 100 on the turn — so a **small bet on river** (e.g., 100) can look like a continuation bet and get called by worse.\n\nAlternatively, go bigger — but risk scaring away callers.\n\nSince opponent has shown weakness, a **modest bet of 100** is likely to get called by second-best hands.\n\nAll-in is excessive — 19,800 — they’ll fold anything weak and call only if they beat you.\n\nSo optimal: **bet 100** to extract value.\n\nFold (-1) or all-in (-2) are suboptimal — you have a strong hand.\n\nCheck (0) forfeits value.\n\nRaise by 100 is reasonable.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong King-high straight, which is likely the best hand given the opponent’s passive play throughout. The board has flush possibilities, but the opponent’s repeated checks make it less likely they have it. Betting 100 maximizes value by getting calls from inferior hands like two pair or a single pair while minimizing risk compared to a larger bet or all-in.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
